---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Getting Started', href: '/docs/getting-started' }, { label: 'Installation' }]}>
  <h1>Installation</h1>
  
  <div class="callout warning">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">⚠️ Content Update in Progress</p>
        <div class="calloutText">This page is being updated to reflect the current OpenFlows installation workflow. The information below may be partially outdated while updates are in progress.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  
  <p>
    OpenFlows is an autonomous software development team orchestrator that runs on your self-hosted Coder deployment. 
    The recommended setup uses Docker Compose to bring up the entire stack including Coder, Redis, and OpenFlows.
  </p>

  <div class="tabTabs">
    <button class="tabTab active" onclick="showInstallTab(0)">Docker Compose (Recommended)</button>
    <button class="tabTab" onclick="showInstallTab(1)">Legacy Methods</button>
  </div>

  <div class="tabContent active" id="install-tab-0">
    <h3>Complete Docker Compose Stack</h3>
    <p>The recommended approach brings up the entire OpenFlows infrastructure with a single command:</p>
    
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/The-AgenticFlow/openflows.git
cd openflows
cp .env.example .env   # edit .env with your GitHub OAuth app credentials

# 1. Start the infrastructure stack
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
    </div>
    
    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="calloutBody">
        <div class="calloutContent">
          <p class="calloutTitle">What This Starts</p>
          <div class="calloutText">
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Coder</strong>: Governed execution environment on port 7080</li>
              <li><strong>Redis</strong>: SharedStore for agent coordination on port 6379</li>
              <li><strong>OpenFlows engine</strong>: Orchestration layer</li>
              <li>All wired together with secure networking</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    
    <h4>Bootstrap OpenFlows</h4>
    <p>After the infrastructure is running, bootstrap OpenFlows:</p>
    
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
      <pre><code># 2. Bootstrap OpenFlows (creates admin, pushes templates, verifies config)
cargo run -p openflows --bin openflows -- bootstrap</code></pre>
    </div>
    
    <h4>Add Your First Tenant</h4>
    <p>A tenant represents a team working on a specific repository:</p>
    
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
      <pre><code># 3. Add a tenant (links GitHub via Coder external auth, creates nexus workspace)
cargo run -p openflows --bin openflows -- tenant add owner/repo --name my-team</code></pre>
    </div>
    
    <h4>Create Your First Issue</h4>
    <p>OpenFlows will now monitor your repository:</p>
    
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
      <pre><code># 4. Create a GitHub issue in your repo — OpenFlows picks it up automatically</code></pre>
    </div>
    
    <div class="callout tip">
      <div class="calloutBody">
        <div class="calloutContent">
          <p class="calloutTitle">Prerequisites</p>
          <div class="calloutText">
            <ul>
              <li>Docker 24+ and Docker Compose</li>
              <li>Git 2.x+</li>
              <li>A GitHub OAuth App for external auth</li>
              <li>At least one LLM provider configured in the Coder dashboard (AI Settings → Coder Agents → Models)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tabContent" id="install-tab-1">
    <h3>Legacy Installation Methods</h3>
    <p>The following methods are deprecated and may not work with the current OpenFlows architecture. Use the Docker Compose method above for the best experience.</p>
    
    <h4>npm (Deprecated)</h4>
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
      <pre><code>npm install -g @the-agenticflow/openflows
openflows --version
openflows-setup
openflows</code></pre>
    </div>
    
    <h4>One-line installer (Deprecated)</h4>
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
      <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/The-AgenticFlow/OpenFlows/main/scripts/install.sh | bash</code></pre>
    </div>
    
    <h4>Cargo (For Development)</h4>
    <p>This method is primarily for developers contributing to OpenFlows:</p>
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
      <pre><code>cargo install openflows
openflows --version</code></pre>
    </div>
    
    <div class="callout warning">
      <div class="calloutBody">
        <div class="calloutContent">
          <p class="calloutTitle">Binary Changes</p>
          <div class="calloutText">The current OpenFlows architecture uses a single <code>openflows</code> binary with subcommands (<code>bootstrap</code>, <code>tenant add</code>) rather than separate binaries like <code>openflows-setup</code>, <code>openflows-dashboard</code>, and <code>openflows-doctor</code>.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    function showInstallTab(index) {
      document.querySelectorAll('#install-tab-0').forEach((el, i) => {
        el.parentElement.querySelectorAll('.tabTab').forEach((btn, j) => {
          btn.classList.toggle('active', j === index);
        });
        el.parentElement.querySelectorAll('.tabContent').forEach((content, j) => {
          content.classList.toggle('active', j === index);
        });
      });
    }
  </script>

  <h2>Environment Configuration</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>.env.example</code> file contains all necessary configuration. Copy it to <code>.env</code> and edit with your specific settings:
  </p>

  <div class="codeBlock">
    <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
    <pre><code># Copy the example environment file
cp .env.example .env

# Edit .env with your GitHub OAuth app credentials and other settings</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h3>Key Configuration Points</h3>
  
  <div class="config-grid">
    <div class="config-item">
      <h4>GitHub OAuth App</h4>
      <p>Required for Coder external authentication. Create a GitHub OAuth App and add the client ID and secret to your <code>.env</code> file.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="config-item">
      <h4>LLM Provider</h4>
      <p>Configure at least one LLM provider in the Coder dashboard (AI Settings → Coder Agents → Models). OpenFlows routes all LLM calls through the Coder AI Gateway.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="config-item">
      <h4>Multi-Tenancy</h4>
      <p>Each team is a "tenant" with its own repository binding. The <code>tenant add</code> command links a GitHub repository to an OpenFlows team.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="config-item">
      <h4>Coder Templates</h4>
      <p>The bootstrap process automatically pushes the <code>openflows-{'{'}role{'}'}</code> templates to your Coder deployment for ephemeral workspace provisioning.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">Architecture-First Approach</p>
        <div class="calloutText">
          OpenFlows encodes architectural discipline through:
          <ul>
            <li><strong>PocketFlow engine</strong>: Declared flow graph for agent coordination</li>
            <li><strong>SharedStore contracts</strong>: Typed Redis schemas for state management</li>
            <li><strong>Node trait pattern</strong>: <code>prep → exec → post</code> separation for each agent</li>
            <li><strong>Explicit routing table</strong>: Recovery built into every step</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Verification</h2>
  <p>After running <code>docker compose up -d</code>, verify all services are running:</p>
  
  <div class="codeBlock">
    <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
    <pre><code># Check Docker containers
docker ps

# Expected output should show:
# - coder (port 7080)
# - redis (port 6379)  
# - openflows engine
# - Other infrastructure services</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>Development Workflow</h2>
  <p>For developers contributing to OpenFlows:</p>
  
  <div class="codeBlock">
    <div class="codeHeader"><span class="codeLang">bash</span></div>
    <pre><code># After code changes, rebuild and update binaries
./update-binaries.sh

# See DEV_BINARY_UPDATE_GUIDE.md for details</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="callout tip">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">Next Steps</p>
        <div class="calloutText">
          After installation and tenant setup:
          <ol>
            <li>OpenFlows will automatically monitor your repository for issues</li>
            <li>Create a GitHub issue to trigger the autonomous team</li>
            <li>Watch as NEXUS picks it up, FORGE writes code, SENTINEL reviews, and VESSEL ships the PR</li>
            <li>LORE documents the changes automatically</li>
          </ol>
          See the <a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting Started</a> guide for a complete walkthrough.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  
  <style>
    .config-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
      gap: 1.5rem;
      margin: 2rem 0;
    }
    
    .config-item {
      background: var(--color-bone);
      border: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);
      border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
      padding: 1.5rem;
      transition: border-color var(--transition-fast), box-shadow var(--transition-fast);
    }
    
    .config-item:hover {
      border-color: var(--color-fog);
      box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
    }
    
    .config-item h4 {
      margin-top: 0;
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
      font-size: var(--font-size-base);
      font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
      color: var(--color-ink);
    }
    
    .config-item p {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
      color: var(--color-graphite);
      line-height: 1.5;
    }
  </style>
</DocsLayout>
